---
import Clip from '../../../components/Clip.astro'
import ClockDoodle from '../../../components/ClockDoodle.astro'
import CodeBlock from '../../../components/CodeBlock.astro'
import Explainer from '../../../components/Explainer.astro'
import { diagrams } from '../../../data/diagrams.js'
import DocsLayout from '../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro'
import { sitePath } from '../../../lib/paths.js'

type Row = readonly [string, string]

const engineRows: Row[] = [
  ['Pictures', 'CEF’s paint callback, as BGRA or I420 frames with a paint ID and a timestamp'],
  ['Sound', 'CEF’s audio stream callback, 48 kHz stereo PCM with the time of its first sample'],
  [
    'Backpressure',
    'Callbacks never wait on a pipe: one frame and 100 audio packets can be pending',
  ],
  ['Not used', 'No screenshot loop, no browser extension and no open debugging port'],
]

const steps = `await suitecut.type(search, 'mechanical keyboard')
await page.keyboard.press('Enter')
await suitecut.highlight(page.locator('h1').first(), { label: 'results' })
await suitecut.narrate('Real typing and a real search. Now open a listing.')
await suitecut.hover(listing)
await suitecut.zoom(page.locator('.ux-image-carousel-item img').first(), { scale: 1.25, holdMs: 1400 })
await suitecut.highlight(price, { label: 'price' })`

const clockRows: Row[] = [
  ['Moving page', 'A 60 fps animation recorded at 60 fps: 241 frames, all 241 new'],
  ['Lower rate', 'The same page at 30 fps: 121 frames; every other paint skipped before copying'],
  ['Still page', 'A static page for 3 s at 30 fps: 1 real frame, then 90 held copies of it'],
  ['Never invented', 'No blending or interpolation; a held frame is counted as held'],
]

const record = `const recording = await browser.startRecording({
  path: 'session.mp4',
  fps: 30,
  audio: true,
})
// ... you and the agent use the window ...
const result = await recording.stop()
// { durationMs, outputFrames, heldFrames, outputBytes, ... }`

const benchmark: readonly (readonly string[])[] = [
  ['Chrome screencast, JPEG, 2x', '54–58', '32–40 ms', '11.7–12.7 s'],
  ['Chrome screencast, PNG, 2x', '53', '41 ms', '12.7 s'],
  ['Shared browser layer hook, 2x', '60', '18.5 ms', '1.7–2.3 s'],
]
---

<DocsLayout
  title="How it works"
  description="The moving parts of suitecut, from the paint callback to the finished MP4, shown with real recordings."
  section="how-it-works"
>
  <h1>How it works</h1>
  <p>
    suitecut is a handful of parts that hand each other frames and sound. This page follows a frame
    from the moment Chromium paints it to the moment it lands in a video or a live stream. Every
    clip on this page is a real recording made by the part it describes.
  </p>

  <h2 id="engine">The engine</h2>
  <p>
    At the centre is Chromium, embedded through CEF. Your program starts it and talks to it over a
    small binary protocol. Nothing reaches in from outside, so there is no debugging port and no
    extension to install.
  </p>
  <Explainer explained={diagrams.engine} />
  <p>
    In the offscreen engine Chromium draws into memory instead of a window, and hands over each
    frame as it paints:
  </p>
  <ul class="dp-rows">
    {
      engineRows.map(([key, value]) => (
        <li>
          <span class="row-name">{key}</span>
          <span class="dp-detail">{value}</span>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>

  <h2 id="steps">Steps become video</h2>
  <p>
    A recording is a script. The cursor, highlights, zooms and captions are steps in it, drawn into
    the page while it records, so nothing is edited afterwards.
  </p>
  <Clip name="ebay" title="Live eBay, recorded and narrated from the script below." sound />
  <CodeBlock code={steps} label="examples/real-sites/ebay.ts" />
  <p>
    A click is real browser input, not a script calling <code>element.click()</code>. The cursor you
    see is drawn by suitecut and moves on the same clock as the page.
  </p>

  <h2 id="clock">One clock</h2>
  <p>
    Chromium only paints when something changes, so frames arrive unevenly. The recorder turns them
    into a steady video: at each output tick it takes the newest real frame. If nothing new came, it
    repeats the last one. If several came, it keeps the newest and skips the others. Sound is cut at
    whole-sample boundaries for each tick, so even 29.97 fps never drifts.
  </p>
  <ClockDoodle />
  <ul class="dp-rows">
    {
      clockRows.map(([key, value]) => (
        <li>
          <span class="row-name">{key}</span>
          <span class="dp-detail">{value}</span>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>
  <p>
    Every recording reports these counts, so you can tell a smooth page from a frozen one without
    watching it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="encode">Encoding</h2>
  <p>
    On macOS a frame stays in GPU memory from paint to encoder. It arrives as an IOSurface, is
    converted to NV12 with Metal and goes to the hardware H.264 encoder (<code
      >h264_videotoolbox</code
    >). Elsewhere, and for other codecs, the same pipeline feeds FFmpeg. Recording and live
    streaming share this pipeline. Only the last step differs: a file, or one or more RTMP
    publishers.
  </p>

  <h2 id="render">Rendering and narration</h2>
  <p>
    Recording writes source clips and a manifest. Rendering is a separate step that picks the
    intervals to keep, lays narration over them and mixes it with the page's own sound. Narration
    comes from Kokoro running locally, so no text leaves your machine.
  </p>
  <Explainer explained={diagrams.recordAndRender} />
  <Clip name="song-maker" title="Chrome Music Lab’s Song Maker. Play it with sound." sound>
    {' '}The tune is the page’s own audio, mixed under the narration.
  </Clip>

  <h2 id="window">Recording a window</h2>
  <p>
    The <a href={sitePath('/docs/shared-browser')}>shared browser</a> is an ordinary window that a person
    uses, so Chromium draws it on screen, not into memory. CEF has no frame callback for a window, so
    suitecut takes the frames macOS is about to show.
  </p>
  <Explainer explained={diagrams.windowFrames} />
  <CodeBlock code={record} />
  <Clip
    name="agent-window"
    title="The shared browser recording itself while an agent works across eBay tabs."
  >
    {' '}The crop follows whichever tab is in front.
  </Clip>
  <p>
    How it compares with the usual way to film Chrome, the DevTools screencast. On the same M4 Mac,
    the same 2x window and a page that redraws every frame for 5 seconds:
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Capture</th>
          <th>Frames per second</th>
          <th>Slowest 5% gap</th>
          <th>Browser CPU time</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          benchmark.map((row) => (
            <tr>
              {row.map((cell) => (
                <td>{cell}</td>
              ))}
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    The page drew about 306 frames in each run. The hook received 301 and added almost nothing to
    the browser's 1.7 s of CPU time with no capture running. The screencast compresses every frame
    to JPEG first, which cost Chrome close to two cores. Hook frames are uncompressed until the
    hardware encoder takes them.
  </p>

  <h2 id="window-sound">Sound from a window</h2>
  <p>
    With <code>audio: true</code>, CEF hands over the front tab's sound. CEF silences a page while
    it captures it, so suitecut only asks for the sound while such a recording runs, and plays it
    back to the speakers itself. The person using the window hears the page as usual.
  </p>
  <Clip
    name="shared-song-maker"
    title="Song Maker in the shared browser, recorded with its sound. Play it with sound."
    sound
  />
  <p>
    The sound's timestamps are moved onto the frame clock. On a test page that flashed and beeped
    together, the beep landed 14 to 19 ms after the flash in the file, well under one frame at 30
    fps.
  </p>

  <h2 id="drive">Driving it</h2>
  <p>
    The TypeScript SDK, the C++ library, the command line and both MCP servers all talk to the same
    native driver. A script, a model and a person can take turns on one page, with the same cookies
    and storage.
  </p>
  <Explainer explained={diagrams.together} />

  <h2 id="live">Going live</h2>
  <p>
    A live stream is the recording pipeline with a publisher at the end. A person can watch a run in
    the live viewer and take control once the agent's current action finishes, then hand it back.
  </p>
  <Explainer explained={diagrams.streaming} />
  <p>
    Next: <a href={sitePath('/docs/extending')}>extending suitecut</a> covers where you can plug your
    own code into each of these parts.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>

<style>
  .row-name {
    color: var(--heading);
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .table-wrap {
    overflow-x: auto;
    margin: var(--space-6) 0;
  }

  table {
    width: 100%;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    font-size: 16px;
    line-height: 24px;
  }

  th,
  td {
    padding: var(--space-2) var(--space-3);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
    text-align: left;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  th {
    color: var(--heading);
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  td:first-child {
    white-space: normal;
  }
</style>
